WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
Court's watch movements cut timing error from minutes per month to seconds per year, while driving

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:11.780
parts cost down to a handful of dollars. This is First Principles Daily, episode 117.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:17.720
Before 1969, a typical mechanical wrist watch movement needed repeated hand adjustment

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:23.500
of its balance wheel and hairspring just to stay within a minute or two per day. A small

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.600
team at Seiko replaced that entire oscillating assembly with a court's crystal vibrating

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:36.120
at 32,768 cycles per second, an integrated circuit to count those cycles and a simple

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.260
stepper motor. The result was an annual error measured in seconds rather than minutes,

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.300
achieved at a parts cost that fell into single-digit dollars instead of the tens or hundreds

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.920
previously required for precision mechanical work. That shift exposed how much of the old

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.460
price had been locked into the assumption that continuous mechanical oscillation and manual

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.940
regulation were unavoidable. Watchmakers had refined the balance wheel

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:06.600
escatement over centuries, treating each successive improvement as an incremental

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.380
tweak to the same basic layout of wheel, spring, and lever.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.040
The convention assumed that accuracy demanded ever finer hand finishing,

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.420
juelling, and regulation of those mechanical parts, so the cost stayed tied to

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.620
skilled labor and the number of separate components that had to mesh.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:29.240
Under that approach, a decent movement might contain dozens of individually machined and

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:35.140
adjusted pieces whose total price reflected both material and the many hours spent fitting and

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:41.320
timing them. Manufacturers accepted this baseline because every prior leap in performance had come

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.760
from polishing the same analog architecture rather than questioning whether continuous mechanical

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:53.140
oscillation was necessary at all. The finished cost therefore remained high, even when the underlying

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:58.160
flying physics of timekeeping only required a stable frequency source and a weight-accounted

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:58.520
cycles.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.760
A typical mechanical movement of that era used a balance wheel whose period depended

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.320
on the stiffness of a hair spring and the mass of the wheel itself.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:13.160
Each unit had to be assembled then observed under a timing machine while tiny weights or

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.360
screws were added or removed until the rate fell inside acceptable limits.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.920
Temperature changes altered the spring's elasticity and the wheel's dimensions, so further compensation

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.760
mechanisms such as bi-metallic curves or special alloys were added.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.540
Each one, another part that required its own fitting step.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.360
Jewel bearings reduced friction at the pivots, yet those jewels still had to be drilled, polished

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.540
and pressed into plates with tolerances measured in microns.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.600
The cumulative effect was that even a mid-grade movement carried an idiot index driven almost

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:52.180
entirely by the sequence of hand operations rather than by the brass, steel, and synthetic

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:58.220
ruby inside it. Because every manufacturer started from the same inherited layout, the industry

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:03.800
treated the resulting labor hours as an immutable feature of accurate timekeeping. The quartz

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.980
approach began by asking what physical property actually delivers a repeatable frequency at

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:17.300
low cost. A quartz crystal slice cut to resonate near 32,768 hertz provides that frequency through

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:22.720
the piezoelectric effect. Once the dimensions and angle are fixed, the frequency is set by the

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:29.080
material itself rather than by ongoing mechanical adjustment. A rough magic wand estimate for the raw

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:34.700
material starts with a few cents worth of cultured quartz, a silicon die for the divider circuit,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.640
and small amounts of copper, steel, and plastic for the motor and case connections.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:45.620
Adding those commodity values yields a floor well below $1 for the core timing elements.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.080
The conventional mechanical movements idiot index sat far higher

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.700
because its finished price reflected assembly labor

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.420
and precision machining far more than the steel, brass, and jewels inside it.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.220
Seiko's redesign eliminated the entire balance wheel,

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:06.700
hairspring and escapement stack by substituting the quartz resonator and electronic divider.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.600
That single substitution removed the need for repeated regulation and the tolerance

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.260
stack up those parts created.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.960
Next, the integrated circuit absorbed the function of multiple mechanical wheels that had

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.200
previously translated oscillations into seconds, minutes, and hours.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.800
The chip replaced those gears with binary counting at negligible added material cost.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.960
The stepper motor then converted the electronic pulses directly into wheel motion, collapsing

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.940
what had been a chain of intermediate levers and pinions.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:43.560
Each of these changes succeeded only after the team solved the supporting engineering problems

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:50.220
of stable crystal mounting, low power CMOS circuitry, and reliable low voltage stepping.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:54.500
But once solved, they removed both mass and labor from the movement.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:59.220
One immediate objection is that a quartz crystal still drifts with temperature.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.320
Yet the redesign and address this by choosing a cut angle whose frequency temperature curve

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.320
is nearly flat around a room temperature rather than by adding compensating mechanisms.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.480
Another concern is that the divider circuit must run for years on a small battery.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:21.080
The move to CMOS transistors cut quiescent current enough that a single silver oxide cell could

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.580
last the better part of a year.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:28.440
The stepper motor itself was sized so its magnetic detent torque exactly matched the load

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.160
of the wheel train, eliminating the need for additional reduction gears that would otherwise

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.920
have multiplied part count. Each consolidation therefore attacked a different slice of the old

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:45.300
idiot index. The crystal removed adjustment labor, the chip removed gear trains, and the motor

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.700
removed intermediate mechanical linkages. The arithmetic is straightforward once the functions

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:56.900
are restated in physical terms. Frequency from atomic spacing, counting from binary logic,

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:03.120
motion from electromagnetic impulse. Production quartz movements soon reached annual errors of

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.140
a few seconds while the parts bill stayed in the low dollar range. A documented improvement

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:14.040
over mechanical predecessors that routinely drifted tens of seconds per day. The new idiot index

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:18.820
sits close to the material floor because the dominant expenses are now the crystal blank,

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:22.860
tank, the tiny circuit, and automated assembly rather than hand-tuning.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:28.420
Trade-offs remain, the quartz crystal still requires temperature compensation or careful

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:33.420
cut angle for best stability, and the battery introduces its own replacement cycle.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:39.040
Those limits are narrower than the old mechanical constraints, yet still set the practical distance

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:40.860
from the absolute physical minimum.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.840
Even after the redesign, the crystal blank must be etched or laser-trimmed to final frequency,

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:51.340
an automated step whose cost is still higher than the raw-cultured quartz

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:56.320
itself. The circuit die must be packaged and bonded, adding another layer

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.220
above the silicon commodity price. The movement must still be cased and tested

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:06.260
for water resistance, operations that sit outside the timing function, yet remain

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:11.140
necessary for the finished product. These residual costs explain why the idiot

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:15.800
index, while dramatically lower than the mechanical case, has not reached the pure

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:22.000
material floor of a few tens of cents. A frequency source whose stability comes from atomic spacing

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:28.000
inside a crystal, rather than from spring tension, shows how replacing an entire class of moving

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:34.880
parts can collapse both cost and adjustment labor at once. Counting cycles with binary logic instead

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:40.680
of gear ratios demonstrates that the counting mechanism itself can be made almost free once the

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:45.900
oscillator is fixed, which other precision mechanisms built around centuries-old analog

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:51.260
linkages might yield similar gains if their underlying physical requirement were restated

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.920
in the language of modern materials and electronics.

